# Deliberately empty: a colours file that carries no palette.
#
# KConfig layers `kdeglobals` under every file it opens, so with nothing in here the style takes
# the active colour scheme -- the same colours it took with no file at all. Measured with
# scripts/probe.py: the window, text, highlight and header colours seen through this style
# are the scheme's with and without this file, and `klassy-dark`, which ships a palette, prints a
# different set, which is the control.
#
# It exists because KSvg asks every theme directory for `colors` on every item it creates and never
# remembers that there was none: with no file, ten to twelve file probes per item on a stock
# session (more with more data directories), and KRunner creates every row again on every
# keystroke; with the file, one. Nothing below this line, on purpose: a [Colors:*] group here would
# be a palette, and the panel would stop following the scheme.
#
# And it must be found by a path with no symbolic link in it, which is why install.sh puts this
# one file in a directory of its own, spelled with every link resolved, and tells the session to
# search there: found through a link, the shell holds two objects for this one file (KConfig
# canonicalises the name on open, the shared lookup compares the name as asked for) and only one
# of them is reparsed when the scheme changes -- measured, the panel's text followed the wallpaper
# and its background did not. On this desktop the home directory is itself reached through a
# link, so the package's own directory, however it is installed, cannot be that path.
